-- title:
--   A principal ideal sheaf on a non-zero-divisor is invertible with trivial dual
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $r \in \Gamma(X, \top)$ be a global section of its structure sheaf, subject to the hypothesis that for every affine open $U$ of $X$ the restriction of $r$ along $U \le \top$ lies in the non-zero-divisors of $\Gamma(X, U)$. Write $I =$ `Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop (Ideal.span {r})` for the quasi-coherent ideal sheaf datum attached to the principal ideal generated by $r$ in $\Gamma(X, \top)$. The conclusion is a conjunction. First, $I$ satisfies the predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $x$ of $X$ there are an affine open $U$, a section $f \in \Gamma(X, U)$ with $x \in X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, and an element $g$ of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f)$ which is a non-zero-divisor there, such that the ideal assigned by $I$ to the affine open $X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f$ is the ideal generated by $g$. Second, the type of isomorphisms between `I.invModule` and the unit object $\mathbf{1}$ of the monoidal category $X.\mathrm{Modules}$ is non-empty; here `I.invModule` is the internal-hom dual $\mathcal{H}om(I.\mathrm{module}, \mathbf{1})$, and `I.module` is the kernel of the map from the unit sheaf of modules to the pushforward of the unit along the closed immersion of the subscheme cut out by $I$.
--
--   This is the statement that the divisor $Z = V(r)$ cut out by a section that is a non-zero-divisor on every affine open is an effective Cartier divisor whose associated line bundle $\mathcal{O}_X(Z) = \mathcal{H}om(I, \mathcal{O}_X)$ is trivial. It is used in the comparison of kernels along the special fibre of the Jacobian model of the modular curve, where $r$ is the pull-back of a uniformiser of the base discrete valuation ring.
